  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nPort Washington is running hot operationally but masked by structural headwinds. Yesterday you ran one groomer and hit $530/groomer\u201413% above your trailing 30-day baseline\u2014despite losing 26% in core grooming revenue. The real story: bather coverage collapsed to zero hours (normally 6.1/day), which constrained groom throughput. Retail exploded 74% and transactions jumped 33%, signaling strong front-of-house energy. Month-to-date you're actually tracking only 7.7% below DOW-adjusted expectation\u2014calendar, not performance, is the gap. You should be proud of this: YTD you're up 43% revenue and appointments versus last year.\n\nHicksville is a concern. Yesterday generated zero grooms and zero bathers\u2014retail only\u2014and you're scheduled for zero groomer capacity today, which means no revenue today either. MTD performance looks fine (+17% vs prior August, only -3.4% vs DOW-adjusted expectation), but the schedule shows structural underutilization: 2.7 groomer-days per day versus Port Washington's 4.2. Bather hours remain at zero across the entire trailing 30 days. This is not a retention problem; this is a capacity problem. You have a 505-person lapse pool at Port Washington alone worth $318K annually\u2014reactivation opportunity, not loss.\n\nOne operational pattern across both stores: bather hours are bottlenecking grooming output. Port Washington's zero bathers yesterday forced single-groomer operation; Hicksville has never ramped bather support. This is your tightest constraint.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Port Washington today 5 groomers + 8.0h bathers; tomorrow 7 groomers + 0.0h bathers. Hicksville today 0 groomers; tomorrow 3 groomers. Next 7 days: 56 groomer-days combined, 76 bather-hours (all Port Washington).**"
